Now run verify. But first, `call?.args[1] as PromptDraft` uses `as` — style guide says avoid `as` to conceal assumptions. Better to validate. Let me avoid the cast: `return call?.args[1]` typed unknown. Could do:

```ts
function promptDraft(backend: FakeBackend): PromptDraft {
  const call = backend.calls.find((entry) => entry.name === 'prompt');
  if (call === undefined) throw new Error('no prompt call recorded');
  return call.args[1] as PromptDraft;
}
```

Still a cast. Alternative: `expect(call?.args[1]).toBe...` no. The style guide allows assertions when justified. But oxlint may not complain. There's a rule? `@typescript-eslint/no-explicit-any` only. Casts are allowed. But to be cleaner, I can type the FakeBackend calls more strongly? Not worth it. Actually I could import `PromptDraft` and use a type guard:

```ts
function promptDraft(backend: FakeBackend): PromptDraft {
  const call = backend.calls.find((entry) => entry.name === 'prompt');
  if (call === undefined) throw new Error('prompt was not called');
  const draft = call.args[1];
  if (typeof draft !== 'object' || draft === null) throw new Error('prompt draft missing');
  return draft as PromptDraft;
