`composer.steer` in `runAction` calls `controller.steer()` — which for empty composer does nothing. The raw alt+return branch in routeComposer is dead code for the configured default keymap because actionFor consumes it first.

The cleanest fix per the design: make the contextual Alt+Enter behavior part of the action path. Change `runAction` case 'composer.steer' to:

```ts
case 'composer.steer':
  if (controller.state.composer.text === '') {
    void controller.promoteNextQueued();
  } else {
    void controller.steer();
  }
  return true;
```

But then the raw branch in routeComposer duplicates this — the keymap can be user-rebound, e.g. user rebinds composer.steer to something else, in which case raw alt+return reaches routeComposer. Having both is consistent if both do the same contextual thing. Alternative: remove the raw alt+return branch entirely and handle it only via the action. But if a user rebinds composer.steer away from alt+enter, then plain alt+return (unbound) → actionFor returns undefined → falls through to routeComposer alt branch → steer(). Keeping both means consistent behavior either way. I'll make routeComposer's raw branch use the same contextual logic (already done) AND change runAction to the same. Even better: factor a helper `altEnter(controller, composer)`? Style guide says avoid single-use helpers; two call sites → a small local helper is justified but inline duplication of a 6-line conditional twice is also OK. Let me just do the conditional inline in both places.

Now — about the flaky/stale line numbers: they shifted because the appended test block in bun's eyes... whatever, the failures are assertions + this key routing issue. After fixing, rerun.

